[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a minute if you need it, then tell me how you'd structure your approach.

[candidate silent 20s]

[candidate · 1:50] Okay, so the puzzle here is that revenue is actually up 15% but margin collapsed from 24% to 6% — that's an 18-point drop, which is huge. So this is almost certainly a cost story, not a demand story, though I want to confirm revenue quality too.

I'd structure it in three buckets. First, decompose revenue: is the 15% growth coming from more stores, more transactions per store, or higher prices? If they opened a lot of new locations, revenue grows but new stores can drag margin while they ramp.

Second, costs — split into COGS (coffee beans, milk, cups) and operating costs (store labor, rent, corporate overhead, marketing). I'd want each as a percent of revenue, then and now, so I can see which line moved.

Third, any one-time or structural changes — new format, delivery apps, discounting.

Can I start with the cost breakdown as a percent of revenue for both years?
