[interviewer · CLARIFY · 1:55] Understood. Before I give you data, you've laid out three buckets. Hold that request; I'll come back to it shortly. Which one do you prioritize first, and why?

[candidate · 3:25] I'd go to costs first. The reason is the arithmetic: revenue grew 15%, so if costs had stayed proportional, margin would have held. The fact that margin fell 18 points while revenue rose means costs grew meaningfully faster than revenue — that's where the 18 points live. Revenue mix matters, but it's more likely an explanatory factor than the root cause.

Within costs, I'd prioritize whichever line is biggest as a share of revenue, because a few points of movement on a 30% line swamps a doubling of a 2% line. For a coffee chain my prior is that store labor and COGS are the two heavyweights, with rent third.

The one way revenue could still be the culprit is if the 15% growth came entirely from new store openings and same-store revenue is flat or down — then I'd be chasing fixed-cost deleverage. So I'd check store count early as a sanity check, but I'd lead with costs.
