[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 5:05] Check your COGS figure and the points it explains against the cost lines I gave you. Coffee beans were 25% of COGS two years ago. Raw coffee bean costs are up 40% over the past two years. Other input costs — dairy, packaging, and food — are up 37.5% over the past two years. Menu prices have not changed in two years.

[candidate · 6:35] You're right, I misread — 42% to 58%, so 16 points, not 13. With overhead's 2 points that's the full 18-point decline. Labor flat. So COGS is essentially the entire story.

Let me check whether input inflation alone explains it. Weighted: beans 25% of COGS up 40%, other 75% up 37.5%. So 0.25 × 40 + 0.75 × 37.5 = 10 + 28.1 = 38.1% increase in input cost per unit.

Now, menu prices flat and store count flat, so the 15% revenue growth is pure volume — they're selling 15% more units. COGS dollars should then be 1.381 × 1.15 = 1.588, up about 59%. And from the ratios: 58/42 × 1.15 = 1.59. Those match.

So there's no waste or mix problem hiding in there — it's entirely input inflation that was never passed through to customers. Two years of 38% cost inflation absorbed with zero price increase.
