[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 6:40] Understood. Beyond what we've discussed so far, what could the client do to reverse the margin decline?

[candidate · 8:10] A few levers beyond pricing, though pricing is the obvious first one.

On the cost side: procurement. They're buying beans at spot exposure apparently — I'd look at forward contracts or hedging green coffee, and multi-year supplier agreements for dairy and packaging. With 200 stores there should be real volume leverage they may not be using.

Second, product mix. Even without raising list prices, you can shift mix toward higher-margin items — espresso drinks and food attach versus straight drip, bundling, loyalty offers that push the higher-margin SKUs.

Third, portion and waste control — standardizing pours, shrinking packaging spec, reducing end-of-day food waste. That won't move 16 points but it could be worth a point or two.

Fourth, overhead — it drifted from 12 to 14 while store count was flat, so something grew there without a volume driver. Worth a look.

Do we have any data on competitor pricing? That would tell me how much headroom there is on the price lever.
