[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 9:46] Understood. You mentioned a price increase that holds volume and costs flat. Walk me through that break-even volume loss you started on, and show me the calculation.

[candidate · 11:16] Sure. Let me set a baseline of revenue = 100 at today's cost structure: COGS 58, labor 22, overhead 14, profit 6.

I need to split variable from fixed. COGS is variable — it scales with cups sold. Labor and overhead I'll treat as fixed in the near term; labor is scheduled shifts, and overhead is corporate. So fixed = 36.

Contribution margin today: 100 − 58 = 42, on revenue of 100.

Now take price up 18% and let volume fall by v. Revenue becomes 118(1−v). COGS becomes 58(1−v), since it tracks units. Contribution = 60(1−v). Fixed stays 36.

Profit = 60(1−v) − 36.

To hold today's profit of 6: 60(1−v) = 42, so 1−v = 0.70, v = 30%.
To merely break even at zero profit: 60(1−v) = 36, 1−v = 0.60, v = 40%.

So they can lose up to 30% of volume and still be no worse off than today. Given same-store volume grew about 16% over two years, demand looks pretty resilient — 30% is a lot of cushion.
