[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 11:21] Volume grew while prices were flat, so does that tell you how customers will respond to a price increase? Walk me through why that growth supports calling the cushion large.

[candidate silent 6s]

[candidate · 12:57] Honestly, it doesn't tell me much. Volume growing at a flat price tells me demand shifted out — more traffic, maybe brand or category tailwind — but it says nothing about the slope of the demand curve. I conflated a shift with elasticity. You can have a growing, highly price-sensitive customer base.

So I'd retract the "cushion is large" claim and just say the cushion is 30 points of volume, and I don't yet know how much of it they'd use. What I'd actually want is a price test: push 10-12% in a sample of stores across different market types, hold the rest as control, and measure transaction counts and ticket over 8-12 weeks. That gives a real elasticity estimate before a chain-wide move.

One thing that does cut in their favor, though it's a prior not evidence — coffee is habitual and the absolute dollar change on a $5 drink is under a dollar.
