[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 13:00] Understood. You've said the cushion is 30 points of volume, and you'd test before moving. Putting it together for the CEO, what is your recommendation, and what would you do first?

[candidate · 14:30] My recommendation: Brew & Bean's margin decline is almost entirely an unpassed-through input cost problem, and the fix is a staged price increase, starting with a test.

The logic: COGS went 42% to 58% of revenue, 16 of the 18 points of margin lost. Labor flat at 22%, overhead up 2. Store count flat at 200, menu prices unchanged in two years, and volume up about 19% — so this isn't demand, footprint, or waste. It's roughly 38% weighted input inflation absorbed entirely by the P&L.

First thing I'd do: launch a price test in 20-30 stores at 10-12%, across urban and suburban markets, measuring transactions and ticket over eight weeks. That gives us elasticity before committing chain-wide, and full restoration needs about 18%, which I wouldn't do in one step.

In parallel, two no-regrets moves: renegotiate bean supply with forward contracts to stop the bleeding, and audit the overhead drift.

Risks: competitor pricing is unknown, and coffee is a repeat purchase, so a visible jump could hurt habit formation. Stage it — two increases, six months apart.
